<h1>How environmental jobs are changing</h1>
<p class="emst-lede">Start with a role. See how its work is shifting, check development gaps on your own device, and explore where the role can lead across federal, state, local, nonprofit and private employers.</p>

<div class="emst-callout emst-callout--warn" role="note">
  <p><strong>Status: draft for NGO-pilot preview ({{ emst.release.date }}).</strong> AI agents checked facts against sources; some claims still await entries in the project's claims register, and records tied to those claims are withheld or marked "Unverified — not for citation". Not yet reviewed by independent human experts. Date-sensitive statements will be re-checked before external release.</p>
  <p><strong>What you are looking at.</strong> This is release {{ emst.release.id }}{% if emst.release.isPreview %}, a preview built from draft records{% endif %}. The competencies, tasks and role profiles were drafted by AI agents (Anthropic's Claude) working to a charter set by the EMST project lead, partly from the project lead's earlier version 1 taxonomy. Practitioners have not reviewed them. No change in any task has yet been assessed by independent human raters, so every "how is this changing" answer says <em>assessment pending</em>. The explorer shows no time shares, importance ratings, critical or needed-at-entry flags, or AI-agent craft ratings. Use it to explore and to give feedback, not to make decisions about people.</p>
</div>

<h2>Three things you can do</h2>
<ul class="emst-cards">
  <li class="emst-card"><h3><a href="{{ '/roles/' | url }}">See how a role is changing</a></h3><p>Tasks, drivers and evidence status for each role variant, with the evidence behind every statement one click down.</p></li>
  <li class="emst-card"><h3><a href="{{ '/gap-check/' | url }}">Check development gaps</a></h3><p>Rate yourself or a team member against observable indicators. Runs only in your browser; you save the result as a file.</p></li>
  <li class="emst-card"><h3><a href="{{ '/pathways/' | url }}">Explore where a role can lead</a></h3><p>Exploratory moves between roles and sectors, with shared competencies and known frictions. Never a recommendation.</p></li>
</ul>

